      "message": "Other than moving this here so that you unpark before doing `parked.addAll(notReady)`, I don\u0027t see any functional difference in the code. What am I missing?\n\nI ran my comment above ^ through an AI reviewer with your patch and it couldn\u0027t find a difference either. Here\u0027s its rewritten comment with some additional details:\n\nOther than moving `ready.unpark()` inside the loop so it runs before `parked.addAll(notReady)`, I don\u0027t see a functional difference. Both before and after this change, when the queue is `[CANCELLED, READY]` we `cancel()` the cancelled task (releasing it to run its cleanup) and `unpark()` the ready one, which is exactly the \"one extra task over the limit\" scenario the commit message describes. What prevents that double-release here? If the intent is that cancelling a task consumes the freed slot, shouldn\u0027t we stop before unparking another (e.g. break/skip the unpark on cancel)?\n\nSeparately: unparking before `parked.addAll(notReady)` re-adds the not-ready tasks leaves a brief window where they\u0027re absent from parked. Since `updateParked()` isn\u0027t synchronized and the unparked task\u0027s `onStop()` can re-enter it, the old ordering (`parked.addAll()` then `ready.unpark()`) seems safer here. Was the reorder deliberate?",
