      "message": "Thanks, and yes, fed PS1 into the AI and it agrees, but it disagrees about the `addAll()` reordering (see below). Everything below is written by AI with light editing from me.\n\n\u003e Yeah, I think you are right, PS1 actually looks like it was correct because we end up breaking after either of the cancels.\n\nAgreed, PS1 correctly `break`s after cancelling a parked task, so the freed slot is consumed by the cancelled task\u0027s cleanup rather than also unparking a ready task — which is the over-limit case the commit describes.\n\nI traced this version through the same scenarios. **This one is the real fix**\n\n## The key difference: the `break`\n\nReformatting the new `updateParked()`:\n\n```java\nfor (ParkedTask ready; (ready \u003d parked.poll()) !\u003d null; ) {\n  if (!ready.cancelIfNeeded()) {              // not cancelled-before-cleanup\n    if (isReadyToStart(ready.task)) {\n      ready.unpark();                          // (B) unpark one ready task\n    } else if (!ready.cancelIfNeeded()) {      // still not cancelled, just not ready\n      notReady.add(ready);\n      continue;                                // skip the break, keep polling\n    }\n  }\n  break;                                       // reached by: cancelled (A/D) OR unparked (B)\n}\nparked.addAll(notReady);\n```\n\nThe only paths that *don\u0027t* `break` are the not-ready ones (`continue`). Every terminal action — unparking a ready task, **or** cancelling a cancelled one — falls through to `break`.\n\n## Why that fixes the bug in PS2\n\nWalk `parked \u003d [CANCELLED, READY]` (one slot just freed by `onStop`):\n\n**Patchset 2**: cancelled task → `cancelIfNeeded()` true → skip body → **no break, loop continues** → polls `READY` → `unpark()`.\n* **2 tasks released** for 1 freed slot. That\u0027s the \"one extra task over the limit,\" so PS2 didn\u0027t actually fix anything.\n\n**Patchset 1**: cancelled task → `cancelIfNeeded()` true → skip body → **`break`**.\n* The `READY` task stays parked. **1 task released.**\n\nThat\u0027s the whole fix. Cancelling a parked task already `close()`s its reservation (decrements core pool size) and interrupts its thread so it resumes and runs its cleanup — consuming the freed slot. So releasing a cancelled task *must not* also unpark a ready one. Patchset 1 enforces that by breaking; patchset 2 didn\u0027t. Case D (cancelled *during* `isReadyToStart()`) behaves the same way — `break`, no extra unpark.\n\n\u003e The reordering was deliberate as it simplifies the code. There is indeed a race there, and I guess your concern is that this makes the race a bit longer? I don\u0027t know if it actually does, because I suspect the compiler can re-order those as it wishes anyway.\n\nAgreed the `break`-based structure reads more cleanly, and you\u0027re right that `updateParked()` is already racy in general — it isn\u0027t synchronized and gets re-entered from `onStop()`.\n\nTwo clarifications though:\n\n**This specific window is newly introduced, not pre-existing.** The hazard I\u0027m flagging is the not-ready tasks being absent from `parked` while a just-unparked task runs. In the original code the order was `addAll(notReady)` *then* `unpark()`, so any re-entrant `updateParked()` triggered by the unparked task\u0027s `onStop()` was guaranteed to see the not-ready tasks back in `parked`. Moving `unpark()` ahead of `addAll` takes that window from deterministically-closed to open. So it\u0027s less \"maybe a bit longer\" and more \"created here.\"\n\n**The compiler would not have reordered these regardless.** `addAll` and `unpark()` aren\u0027t plain field accesses the JIT can freely shuffle — both perform synchronization actions: `PriorityBlockingQueue.offer()` takes its internal `ReentrantLock`, `unpark()` -\u003e `close()` -\u003e `incrementCorePoolSizeBy(-1)` is `synchronized`, and `latch.countDown()` is an AQS volatile write. Under the JMM (JLS 17.4) synchronization actions have a total order consistent with program order, and same-thread synchronization actions can\u0027t be reordered relative to each other in an observable way. So the source ordering is what pinned `addAll` before `unpark`; the optimizer wouldn\u0027t have changed it.\n\nImpact is admittedly small — the unparked task would have to fully run and complete its `onStop()` -\u003e `updateParked()` in the gap, and even then the not-ready tasks stay parked and get their next chance on the following `onStop()`. So it\u0027s a tiny-probability self-healing missed-wakeup, not a safety/data-loss bug.\n\nWe can keep the clean structure and still close the window at ~no cost by capturing the ready task and unparking after `addAll`:\n\n```java\nParkedTask ready \u003d null;\nfor (ParkedTask p; (p \u003d parked.poll()) !\u003d null; ) {\n  if (!p.cancelIfNeeded()) {\n    if (isReadyToStart(p.task)) {\n      ready \u003d p;\n    } else if (!p.cancelIfNeeded()) {\n      notReady.add(p);\n      continue;\n    }\n  }\n  break;\n}\nparked.addAll(notReady);\nif (ready !\u003d null) {\n  ready.unpark();\n}\n```\n\nCan we do that rather than accept the widened window? If you\u0027d still rather take it as-is given how improbable the trigger is, I won\u0027t block on it — I just wanted to flag that the window is new and the reorder isn\u0027t something the compiler would have done on its own.",
